 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-- Time complexity depends on the number of cells and the density of high-value cells. Slicing is $O(N)$ for $N$ cells, but with higher constant factors due to intersection calculations. Splatting is also $O(N)$ but with lower overhead per cell [S1].
-- Memory complexity is $O(N)$ to store cell data and intermediate structures [S1].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-- Performance is suitable for large datasets requiring parallel processing, as parts of the method can be parallelized [S1].
-- Specific hardware and resolution metrics are not given in the sources.
-
-**Scaling:**
-- Scales well with parallel processing for large datasets [S1].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-- **Strengths:** Reduces aliasing in sparse regions; handles unstructured grids effectively; balances accuracy and speed [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the threshold; may introduce discontinuities at the threshold boundary if not handled smoothly [G].
-
-**When to Use:**
-- Use for large unstructured or curvilinear grid datasets where both high-fidelity surfaces and sparse internal structures need to be visualized [S1].
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-- Not ideal for purely structured grids where simpler methods suffice, or when real-time performance is critical and the threshold tuning overhead is prohibitive [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** Depends on the distribution of cells relative to threshold $T$. Polygonal rendering is $O(N_p \cdot F)$ where $N_p$ is the number of polygonal cells and $F$ is the number of faces. Splatting is $O(N_s \cdot K)$ where $N_s$ is the number of splatted cells and $K$ is the kernel size.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store cell data and render queues.
+- **Measured Performance:** Interactive visualization on large datasets is supported, especially when parallelized [S1]. Performance scales with the ratio of high-value to low-value cells.
+- **Strengths:** Provides sharp boundaries for significant features while maintaining anti-aliasing for less significant regions. Efficient for large datasets.
+- **Limitations:** Visual discontinuities at the threshold boundary if not carefully tuned. Requires careful selection of threshold $T$.
+- **When to Use:** When visualizing large unstructured/curvilinear grids where both precise boundaries and smooth anti-aliasing are required.
+- **When Not to Use:** When uniform precision is required across all values, or when the threshold classification introduces unacceptable visual artifacts.
 
 ## Background
-**Origin:**
-- The method was described in the paper "Volume Rendering for Curvilinear and Unstructured Grids" by Silva et al. [S1].
-- It builds on earlier work by Yagel et al. for slicing [S1] and Zwicker et al. for anti-aliased splatting [S1].
-
-**History and Development:**
-- The method was developed to address the limitations of pure slicing and splatting methods for unstructured grids [S1].
-- It was part of a broader effort to enable interactive visualization of large scientific datasets [S1].
-
-**Where it is Used:**
-- Used in scientific visualization for curvilinear and unstructured grids [S1].
+Originated in the context of volume rendering for curvilinear and unstructured grids, proposed by Silva et al. in 2003 [S1]. It builds on earlier work by Yagel et al. for polygonal slicing [S1] and Zwicker et al. for anti-aliased ellipsoidal splatting [S1]. The method was developed to address the limitations of pure polygonal or pure splatting approaches for large, complex datasets.
 
